<!-- ============================================================
     Microsoft SharePoint Server Missing Authentication EoP
     CVE-2026-56164 (CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function)
     Affected: SharePoint Server (multiple supported versions)
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK:
       T1190 - Exploit Public-Facing Application (TA0001 Initial Access)
       T1098 - Account Manipulation (unauthenticated privilege grant via REST API)
       T1548 - Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism (auth bypass enabling EoP)
       T1136 - Create Account (account creation via unauthenticated admin endpoint)
     CISA KEV: Added 2026-07-14, Federal Deadline 2026-07-17
     Rule IDs: 103925-103934
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-14

     Vulnerability Summary:
       CVE-2026-56164: SharePoint Server fails to enforce authentication
       for critical administrative REST API endpoints. An unauthenticated
       network attacker can call privileged SharePoint REST endpoints
       (/_api/web/SiteGroups, /_api/web/RoleAssignments,
       /_api/web/SiteAdministrators) without credentials and receive 2xx
       responses. This allows the attacker to add accounts to privileged
       SharePoint groups, assign full-control role bindings, or grant
       site administrator access without any valid credentials.

       Exploit sequence:
         1. Attacker POSTs to /_api/contextinfo to obtain a form digest
            token (anonymous access succeeds due to missing auth check).
         2. Attacker uses the form digest to POST to a privileged endpoint
            such as /_api/web/SiteGroups(n)/Users or
            /_api/web/RoleAssignments/addroleassignment, adding their
            account to the Owners or Site Administrators group.
         3. Windows Security Event 4728 fires with the SharePoint AppPool
            account as the Subject, confirming web-driven privilege grant.

     Log source prerequisites:
       HTTP layer:
         - Traefik access logs in JSON format forwarded to Wazuh.
           Field names follow Traefik defaults: RequestMethod, RequestPath,
           RequestHost, DownstreamStatus, ClientHost.
       Windows Security Event layer:
         - Windows Security Event Log forwarded to Wazuh. Requires
           Security audit policy: Audit Account Management (Success).
           Wazuh must be running the Windows Security log decoder.
           Fields: win.system.eventID, win.eventdata.subjectUserName,
           win.eventdata.targetUserName, win.eventdata.memberName.

     Detection layers:
       1. HTTP (rules 103925-103929): Traefik JSON access logs covering
          requests to privileged SharePoint REST endpoints. Success (2xx)
          on these endpoints is the primary exploit delivery signal.
          Rule 103925 anchors on endpoint path; 103926-103929 grade
          severity by endpoint sensitivity and success status.
       2. Windows Security Events (rules 103930-103934): Group membership
          changes (EventID 4728) and account creation (EventID 4720)
          attributed to IIS AppPool or SharePoint service accounts indicate
          the web application executed unauthorized privilege changes in
          response to an unauthenticated exploit request.

     Severity tiers:
        9 - Any request to privileged SharePoint REST endpoint (info anchor)
       11 - POST to /_api/contextinfo returning 2xx (anon form digest obtained)
       12 - 2xx response to SiteGroups or RoleAssignments (privilege manipulation)
       13 - POST to SiteGroups(n)/Users returning 2xx (group member addition)
       14 - POST to SiteAdministrators or addroleassignment returning 2xx
            (site admin or full-control grant confirmed; near-certain exploit)
       Windows Event layer:
       12 - EventID 4728 targeting SharePoint admin group name patterns
       13 - EventID 4728 with IIS/SharePoint service account as Subject
       12 - EventID 4720 with IIS/SharePoint service account as Subject

     False positive guidance:
       103925: SharePoint admins legitimately call these REST APIs from
         management tools; correlate with 103926-103929 for 2xx responses.
       103926: A POST to contextinfo during normal site load is expected
         for authenticated sessions; flag only unexpected sources or clients
         without prior authenticated sessions.
       103927-103929: 2xx on admin REST endpoints from non-admin sources is
         anomalous; SharePoint returns 401/403 to unauthenticated callers
         under normal operation.
       103931: SharePoint admins add members via the web UI; review Subject
         account. If Subject is a human admin account this is expected.
       103932: Flag immediately; IIS AppPool accounts should not trigger
         Windows group membership changes directly.
       103934: Same as 103932 for account creation events.
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="initial_access,privilege_escalation,exploit_pubfacing,sharepoint,cve_2026_56164,cisa_kev,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 1: HTTP / Traefik reverse proxy (JSON access logs)
       Rule IDs: 103925-103929

       CVE-2026-56164 is exploited by sending requests to SharePoint
       administrative REST API endpoints without authentication. The
       missing auth check causes the server to process these requests
       and return 2xx responses. Monitoring the reverse proxy layer
       catches exploit delivery before post-exploit host events fire.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Base anchor: any request to privileged SharePoint REST or SOAP endpoint.
       Covers the primary REST endpoints used in CVE-2026-56164 exploitation:
         /_api/web/SiteGroups       - site group membership management
         /_api/web/RoleAssignments  - permission role bindings
         /_api/web/SiteAdministrators - site administrator management
         /_vti_bin/UserGroup.asmx   - legacy SOAP user/group management
         /_api/SP.Directory.*       - directory services API
         /_layouts/15/user.aspx     - user management UI page
       All child rules (103926-103929) chain from this anchor via if_sid. -->
  <rule id="103925" level="9">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:/_api/web/(?:sitegroups|roleassignments|siteadministrators)|/_vti_bin/(?:usergroup|lists)\.asmx|/_api/SP\.Directory\.|/_layouts/15/user\.aspx)</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-56164: Request to privileged SharePoint REST endpoint $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost)  -  potential missing-auth EoP exploit delivery (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_56164,initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST to /_api/contextinfo returning 2xx.
       The contextinfo endpoint issues a form digest value required for
       all state-changing POST requests in SharePoint. Under normal
       operation this endpoint requires a valid authentication session.
       An unauthenticated POST that returns 2xx indicates the auth check
       is missing and the attacker has obtained a valid form digest token,
       which is the first step in the CVE-2026-56164 exploit chain. -->
  <rule id="103926" level="11">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/_api/contextinfo</field>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-56164: POST to /_api/contextinfo from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost) returned $(DownstreamStatus)  -  anonymous form digest token obtained; first step in unauthenticated privilege escalation exploit chain (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_56164,initial_access,anon_token_grant,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- 2xx response to SiteGroups or RoleAssignments endpoint.
       SiteGroups controls SharePoint group membership (Owners, Members,
       Visitors). RoleAssignments controls permission levels (Full Control,
       Design, Contribute). A 2xx response to either endpoint confirms the
       server processed the request. Under normal operation these endpoints
       require authentication; a 2xx from an unexpected or unauthenticated
       caller indicates active CVE-2026-56164 exploitation. -->
  <rule id="103927" level="12">
    <if_sid>103925</if_sid>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/_api/web/(?:sitegroups|roleassignments)</field>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-56164: $(RequestMethod) to $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) returned $(DownstreamStatus)  -  SiteGroups or RoleAssignments access succeeded; privilege manipulation via missing-auth exploit (T1190/T1098)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1098</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_56164,privilege_escalation,group_manipulation,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST 2xx to SiteGroups users sub-resource: group member addition.
       POSTing to /_api/web/SiteGroups(id)/Users adds a principal to a
       SharePoint site group. A 2xx response confirms the group membership
       change was accepted. This is the direct privilege escalation action:
       the attacker adds their account to the site Owners or other
       privileged group without authentication. -->
  <rule id="103928" level="13">
    <if_sid>103925</if_sid>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/_api/web/sitegroups\(\d+\)/users</field>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-56164 HIGH: POST to SiteGroups users at $(RequestHost)$(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) returned $(DownstreamStatus)  -  group member added without authentication; active privilege escalation via CVE-2026-56164 (T1190/T1098)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1098</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_56164,privilege_escalation,group_member_add,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST 2xx to SiteAdministrators, addroleassignment, or UserGroup.asmx.
       SiteAdministrators is the top-level privilege tier in a SharePoint
       site collection. addroleassignment grants any permission level
       including Full Control. UserGroup.asmx is the legacy SOAP endpoint
       for user and group management that mirrors the REST API surface.
       A successful POST to any of these endpoints means the attacker
       achieved site-administrator-level access without credentials. -->
  <rule id="103929" level="14">
    <if_sid>103925</if_sid>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:/_api/web/siteadministrators|/_vti_bin/usergroup\.asmx|/_api/web/roleassignments/addroleassignment)</field>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-56164 CRITICAL: POST to admin endpoint $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) returned $(DownstreamStatus)  -  site administrator or full-control role granted without authentication; immediate incident response required (T1190/T1098/T1548)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1098</id>
      <id>T1548</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_56164,privilege_escalation,admin_grant,critical,high_confidence,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 2: Windows Security Events
       Rule IDs: 103930-103934
       Log source: Windows Security Event Log forwarded to Wazuh.
       Requires: Security audit policy Audit Account Management (Success).
       Wazuh base SID 60103 covers Windows Security log events.

       After CVE-2026-56164 exploitation, the privilege grant appears in
       the Windows Security Event Log as standard account management events.
       The key indicator is that the Subject performing the change is an
       IIS AppPool account or SharePoint service account, not a human admin.
       IIS AppPool accounts trigger Windows group changes only when the
       web application itself initiates the operation, which occurs when
       an unauthenticated HTTP request reaches the unprotected admin endpoint.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Base anchor: Windows Security Event 4728.
       EventID 4728 fires when a member is added to a security-enabled
       global group. SharePoint site group memberships are backed by Windows
       security groups whose names follow SharePoint naming conventions.
       This anchor enables child rules to filter by group name and Subject. -->
  <rule id="103930" level="0">
    <if_sid>60103</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4728$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-56164: Base anchor - member added to security-enabled global group (EventID 4728)</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- EventID 4728 targeting SharePoint admin group name patterns.
       SharePoint creates Windows security groups using names that include
       keywords such as SharePoint, WSS_ADMIN_WPG, WSS_WPG, Farm Administrators,
       SP_Admin, or SPWA. A member addition to any of these groups should
       be reviewed, particularly when the Subject is not a human admin. -->
  <rule id="103931" level="12">
    <if_sid>103930</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetUserName" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:SharePoint|WSS_ADMIN|WSS_WPG|Farm.Admin|SP_Admin|SPWA)</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-56164: Member $(win.eventdata.memberName) added to SharePoint admin group $(win.eventdata.targetUserName) by $(win.eventdata.subjectUserName)  -  potential unauthenticated privilege grant; correlate with HTTP exploit events on $(agent.name) (T1098)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_56164,privilege_escalation,group_manipulation,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- EventID 4728 where Subject is IIS AppPool or SharePoint service account.
       When the account initiating a Windows group membership change is an
       IIS AppPool identity, IUSR, or a named SharePoint service account
       (spservice, sp_farm, svc_sp, etc.), the change was made by the web
       application process, not by a human administrator. This indicates the
       SharePoint IIS worker process responded to an unauthenticated HTTP
       request by executing a privilege escalation operation. This is the
       highest-confidence Windows-layer signal for CVE-2026-56164 exploitation. -->
  <rule id="103932" level="13">
    <if_sid>103930</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.subjectUserName" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:IIS APPPOOL\\|w3wp|IUSR|spservice|spadmin|sp_farm|sp_service|sharepoint_service|svc_sp)</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-56164 HIGH: Group membership change in $(win.eventdata.targetUserName) initiated by IIS/SharePoint service account $(win.eventdata.subjectUserName) on $(agent.name)  -  web process executed privilege grant; likely CVE-2026-56164 unauthenticated EoP (T1098/T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_56164,privilege_escalation,service_account_exploit,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Base anchor: Windows Security Event 4720.
       EventID 4720 fires when a user account is created. SharePoint can
       provision or enable accounts as part of site membership flows.
       An account creation event where the Subject is a SharePoint or
       IIS service account indicates the web application created the account,
       which can occur as a post-exploit action following CVE-2026-56164
       to establish a persistent backdoor account with elevated access. -->
  <rule id="103933" level="0">
    <if_sid>60103</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4720$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-56164: Base anchor - user account created (EventID 4720)</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- EventID 4720 where Subject is IIS AppPool or SharePoint service account.
       Account creation triggered by an IIS AppPool or SharePoint service
       account identity indicates the SharePoint web process itself issued
       the account creation call. Under normal operation human administrators
       use the admin console to create accounts; creation attributed to a
       service process identity is a strong indicator of post-exploit
       backdoor account provisioning via CVE-2026-56164. -->
  <rule id="103934" level="12">
    <if_sid>103933</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.subjectUserName" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:IIS APPPOOL\\|w3wp|IUSR|spservice|spadmin|sp_farm|sp_service|sharepoint_service|svc_sp)</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-56164: User account $(win.eventdata.targetUserName) created by IIS/SharePoint service account $(win.eventdata.subjectUserName) on $(agent.name)  -  unauthenticated account provisioning via missing-auth exploit; possible backdoor account (T1136/T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_56164,privilege_escalation,account_creation,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
